## 1. Executive Summary

### The gap

Existing video encoding benchmarks evaluate providers through a streaming lens — adaptive bitrate ladders, segment-based delivery, and startup latency. No benchmark exists for **quality-optimized single-file encoding**: the use case that matters for archival storage, e-commerce product videos, UGC platforms, and online education.

### What we tested

{{ stats.n_clips }} video clips across four industry verticals, encoded through {{ stats.n_providers }} providers using AV1 and H.265, measured on VMAF perceptual quality, file size, and compression efficiency.

### Headline findings

1. **Compression efficiency varies significantly across providers.** At equivalent quality (VMAF {{ vmaf_transparent }}), file sizes differ by up to {{ "%.0f" | format(stats.max_size_spread_pct) }}% between the most and least efficient provider.
2. **{{ stats.best_provider_at_target }} produces the smallest files at VMAF {{ vmaf_transparent }}**, averaging {{ "%.1f" | format(stats.best_size_at_target_mb) }} MB across all test clips. The next-closest provider requires {{ "%.1f" | format(stats.second_size_at_target_mb) }} MB — a {{ "%.0f" | format(stats.size_diff_pct) }}% difference.
3. **AV1 delivers {{ "%.0f" | format(stats.av1_h265_advantage_pct) }}% better compression than H.265** across all providers. {{ stats.av1_winner_note }}
4. **Ease of use and compression efficiency present different tradeoffs.** {{ stats.ease_efficiency_note }}

